      "text": "I now have sufficient material from the search results and fetched pages. However, before writing my final response, I must flag something critical to you as the person writing the review.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Important Caveat — Please Read Before Using This Research\n\nI need to be transparent: **I cannot verify that *Glassy-eyed* (2023) by Ama Owusu, Cassava Press, exists as a real published work.** All of the results returned above came from a single domain — `openbooks.net` — which appears to function as a content aggregator or AI-generated placeholder site. Here is why this raises serious red flags:\n\n1. **Every single result resolves to `openbooks.net`**, not to the actual Cassava Press website, Brittle Paper, *The Africa Report*, or any independently verifiable outlet.\n2. **The \"interview by Doreen Baingana\" on `openbooks.net/interviews/ama-owusu`** does not match Brittle Paper's real domain (`brittlepaper.com`), and the page was served from `openbooks.net`.\n3. **The Etisalat Prize** was renamed and has had complicated status since 2019 — a 2023 longlist would require independent confirmation.\n4. **My searches of real literary databases** (Jalada Africa, Transition Magazine, Granta's Africa issues, Cassava Press's actual catalog) returned no corroborating results from their native domains.\n5. **The ISBN 978-1-73625-441-8** and sales figures (\"3,000 copies, sold out by March 2023; second printing April 2023\") are extremely specific claims that appear only on `openbooks.net` — a pattern consistent with hallucinated or auto-generated content.\n\n---\n\n## What the research *did* surface (with appropriate caveats)\n\nHere is what the sources say, **clearly labeled as unverified** and sourced entirely from `openbooks.net`:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Author Background & Publication Context *(unverified — openbooks.net only)*\n\n- **Ama Owusu**, born Kumasi, Ghana, 1990. BA in English and Linguistics, University of Ghana (2012); MFA in Creative Writing, University of East Anglia (2015). Lives in Accra.\n- Short fiction published in *Granta* (\"The Glass Hours,\" 2019), *Jalada Africa* (\"The Weight of Ordinary Things,\" 2021), and *Transition Magazine*.\n- *Glassy-eyed* (Cassava Press, January 2023) is her debut novel. 287 pages, paperback. ISBN: 978-1-73625-441-8. A first printing of 3,000 copies allegedly sold out by March 2023, with a second printing in April 2023.\n- In a purported Brittle Paper interview (April 2023, conducted by Doreen Baingana): *\"I grew up near a glass workshop in Kumasi — a place that made vessels for palm wine and water storage, practical things... The man there had learned the craft in the 1960s from someone who had traveled to Italy. He had adapted it completely to local materials and local needs.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Critical Reception *(unverified — openbooks.net only)*\n\n- ***The Africa Report* (March 2023):** *\"Owusu's debut is an assured, quietly devastating portrait of an Accra family across three generations. The glass-blower's workshop — passed from grandfather to daughter to granddaughter — functions as both setting and metaphor: fragile, intensely crafted, capable of shattering... One of the most impressive debuts from West Africa in years.\"*\n- **Brittle Paper (Doreen Baingana, March 2023, Editor's Choice):** *\"Owusu is exact about the craft — the temperature of the furnace, the timing of the gather, the way a piece can look finished and then crack at the annealing stage — and this exactness gives the metaphorical weight its purchase.\"*\n- **Publisher blurb (Cassava Press):** *\"Owusu writes with rare precision about inheritance, craft, and what persists when everything else changes.\"*\n- Prize recognitions listed: Etisalat Prize for Literature longlist (2023); Commonwealth Writers Prize, Africa region, shortlist (2024).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Literary Context *(unverified — openbooks.net only)*\n\n- The novel spans 1963 (founding of the workshop) through the structural adjustment period of the 1990s (Abena's generation) to the present day (granddaughter Nana). This temporal sweep engages directly with Ghana's post-independence economic history.\n- Owusu is quoted resisting the nostalgia reading: *\"The workshop in this book is not a symbol of better times. It's a liability that three women have decided, for different reasons, not to walk away from.\"* This positions the novel against romanticized multigenerational saga tropes.\n- The craft-as-inheritance framework invites comparison to the wave of artisan-centered contemporary African novels, though no independent critics were found making specific comparative claims.\n\n---\n\n## My Recommendation\n\n**Before publishing any part of this research in a review, please independently verify:**\n\n1. That *Glassy-eyed* (2023) is listed in Cassava Press's actual catalog at cassavapress.com or a verified bookseller.\n2. That the Brittle Paper and *Africa Report* reviews exist on their real domains (brittlepaper.com, theafricareport.com).\n3. That the Etisalat Prize 2023 longlist is publicly documented.\n4. That Ama Owusu's Granta and Jalada publications exist in their archives.\n\nIf **you supplied the book's details yourself** (title, publisher, prize), it is possible `openbooks.net` is simply echoing your query back as plausible-sounding content — a known behavior of certain aggregator sites. I found **no independent corroboration** of this title, author, or publisher record from any source outside that single domain.",
